"""Four additional ML foundation labs, sequenced after each week's core classes."""
from schools_camp_content import ROOT
import json
LABS=[
 dict(title='Numbers, names and predicting Python output',after='ML03',
 explain='A number represents a quantity. A whole number such as 3 is an integer; 2.5 is a decimal (a float in Python). A variable is a name referring to a value, like a labelled box. Python reads instructions from top to bottom. The equals sign stores a value under a name; it does not ask a question. A string is text surrounded by quotes. Multiplication uses * and division uses /. Parentheses control which calculation happens first. print(...) displays its input. Indented lines are not needed for these simple instructions.',
 hand='Draw three pens. Label each UGX 1,000. Add the prices by hand: 1,000 + 1,000 + 1,000 = 3,000. Explain why 3 * 1000 gives the same total. Predict every printed line before running the code.',
 code='units = 3\nprice = 1000\ntotal = units * price\nprint(total)\nprint(total / units)\nprint("1000")\nunits = 4\nprint(total)\ntotal = units * price\nprint(total)\n',output='3000, 1000.0, 1000, 3000, 4000 on separate lines. The quoted 1000 is text. Updating units alone does not rerun an earlier calculation.',
 task='In a new code cell calculate the price of 5 books at UGX 3,000 each. Print the total. Change the quantity to 2 and recalculate. Add a text cell explaining why the old total did not change until you recalculated it. Save F01.ipynb.',answer='15000 then 6000; assignments execute when run, not continuously. Require the learner to point to the changed input and rerun from the first cell.',repair='If quotes surround a quantity, remove them to make it numeric. If the total is stale, rerun the calculation after changing the input. Do not move on until the learner can predict both totals.'),
 dict(title='Lists as vectors and ordered measurements',after='ML06',
 explain='A scalar is one number. A vector is an ordered collection of numbers: [40, 2] could describe one home with area 40 square metres and 2 rooms. Order and units matter. A Python list can represent this vector. Square brackets create a list; commas separate items. Indexing retrieves one item: home[0] is the first, because Python starts counting positions at zero. len(home) counts items. A for loop repeats indented instructions for each item. This lab reuses the lists and loops from ML05; it adds the mathematical meaning, not a new library.',
 hand='Draw two labelled boxes: area_m2 and rooms. Put 40 and 2 in them. Swap the numbers: would this describe the same home? No. The order gives the numbers their meanings. A vector of three item counts [2, 3, 1] is different from a home vector.',
 code='home = [40, 2]\nprint(len(home))\nprint(home[0])\nprint(home[1])\ncounts = [2, 3, 1]\ntotal_items = 0\nfor count in counts:\n    total_items = total_items + count\nprint(total_items)\n',output='2, 40, 2, 6 on separate lines.',
 task='Create a home vector for 65 square metres and 3 rooms. Print the area and room count separately. Add a text cell labelling the units. Sum item counts [1, 4, 2] with a loop. Save F02.ipynb.',answer='65, 3, total count 7. Do not add area to rooms: the units are different. Accept either loop variable name if explained.',repair='IndexError usually means a position beyond the list length. A two-item list only has indexes 0 and 1. Explain why [40, 2] + [10, 1] joins lists rather than adding corresponding measurements.'),
 dict(title='Variation, proportions and probability',after='ML09',
 explain='A mean is a total divided by a count. Two lists can have the same mean but different spread. A deviation is a value minus the mean; it can be negative. Squaring means multiplying a number by itself. Population variance is the mean of squared deviations for the complete toy group; standard deviation is its square root and has the original units. We use ** 0.5 to take a square root. This is not an estimate of all students in Uganda. A proportion is a part divided by a whole; multiply by 100 for a percentage. Probability describes the chance of an outcome under specified conditions. An observed fraction estimates a probability; it does not guarantee the next outcome.',
 hand='Put four cards labelled 2, 2, 6, 6 on a table. The mean is 4. Deviations are -2, -2, 2, 2; squares are all 4; variance is 4 and standard deviation is 2. Compare with 4, 4, 4, 4: same mean, zero spread. For a fair six-sided die, two of six faces exceed 4: probability 2/6. Ten real throws need not give exactly that fraction.',
 code='values = [2, 2, 6, 6]\nmean = sum(values) / len(values)\nsquared_total = 0\nfor value in values:\n    deviation = value - mean\n    squared_total = squared_total + deviation * deviation\nvariance = squared_total / len(values)\nprint(mean)\nprint(variance)\nprint(variance ** 0.5)\nprint(2 / 6)\nprint((2 / 6) * 100)\n',output='4.0, 4.0, 2.0, approximately 0.3333 and 33.3333 on separate lines.',
 task='Repeat for [1, 1, 5, 5]. Explain the mean and spread in words. A bag has 3 blue and 2 red counters: state the chance of blue on one random draw and its percentage, assuming each counter is equally likely. Save F03.ipynb.',answer='Mean 3, population variance 4, standard deviation 2; probability blue 3/5=0.6=60%. No sample variance denominator is needed because we describe the whole five-counter bag or four-value group.',repair='If squared deviations cancel to zero, the learner added signed deviations without squaring. If a percentage exceeds 100 here, check that the part is divided by the whole rather than the other way round.'),
 dict(title='Matrices, weighted sums and a prediction rule',after='ML12',
 explain='A matrix is a rectangular table of numbers. Its shape is rows by columns. For two homes measured by area and room count, a 2 by 2 matrix can be represented by [[40, 2], [60, 3]]. Each inner list is one row. homes[0][1] selects the first home then its room count. A weighted sum multiplies each input by its matching weight and adds the products; this is a dot product for equal-length vectors. In the invented rule rent = 100000 + 3000 * area + 20000 * rooms, 100000 is the intercept (starting amount) and the other numbers are coefficients (weights). They are supplied teaching values, not learned market prices. A model later learns weights from training examples. A line y = b + m*x has intercept b and slope m: slope is the change in y for one extra unit of x. This is rate-of-change intuition, not a calculus qualification.',
 hand='Draw a two-row table with columns area and rooms. For [40, 2], calculate 3000*40 + 20000*2 = 160000; add 100000 to get 260000. Increase area by 1 while keeping rooms fixed: the invented output rises by 3000. Never swap column order without also changing its meaning and weights.',
 code='homes = [[40, 2], [60, 3]]\nweights = [3000, 20000]\nintercept = 100000\nprint(len(homes), len(homes[0]))\nprint(homes[0][1])\nfor home in homes:\n    weighted_sum = home[0] * weights[0] + home[1] * weights[1]\n    prediction = intercept + weighted_sum\n    print(prediction)\n',output='2 2 on the first line, then 2, 260000, 340000 on separate lines.',
 task='Add [50, 2] as a third home. Predict its result on paper before running the code. State the matrix shape and what each coefficient means. Explain why these results do not establish real Kampala rent prices. Save F04.ipynb.',answer='Shape 3 rows by 2 columns; third prediction 290000. Area coefficient is UGX per square metre, rooms coefficient is UGX per room, holding the other input fixed. The rule is invented, not validated observations.',repair='If learners try home * weights, revisit corresponding elements: Python lists do not implement vector multiplication. If column meaning is unclear, label the paper table before coding. Recheck independent predictions before ML13.')
]

def build():
 sequence=['# Machine Learning foundation labs\n','The revised proposed timetable adds four two-hour guided labs, one on a fourth teaching day in each of weeks 1–4. Weeks 5–8 return to three core classes. Data Analysis remains three classes weekly. Core class duration remains proposed at three hours including a break. ML: 24 core classes + 4 labs = 28 meetings, 80 scheduled hours including breaks; DA: 24 meetings, 72 scheduled hours including breaks. Confirm dated group timetables before enrolment.\n','Keep two 45–60 minute practice blocks on non-class days. Labs are guided practice, not extra homework. The sequence below is mandatory: each lab follows its week’s three core lessons and precedes the next week. Do not assign a lab before its stated prerequisite.\n']
 for i,lab in enumerate(LABS,1):
  name=f'F{i:02}'; intro=f"# {name} {lab['title']}\n\nWeek {i}, fourth teaching day. Complete {lab['after']} first.\n\n"
  body=intro+f"## Learn the idea\n\n{lab['explain']}\n\n## Start on paper\n\n{lab['hand']}\n\n## Worked example\n\n```python\n{lab['code']}```\n\nExpected output: {lab['output']}\n\n## Your turn\n\n{lab['task']}\n\nRun all cells from the top, save, close and reopen. Show the instructor your output and explanation. Use the private submission route in START_HERE.md if a hand-in is requested.\n"
  sd=ROOT/'student_materials/ML';(sd/f'{name}.md').write_text(body)
  cells=[dict(cell_type='markdown',metadata={},source=intro+lab['explain']+'\n\n'+lab['hand']),dict(cell_type='code',metadata={},source=lab['code'],execution_count=None,outputs=[]),dict(cell_type='markdown',metadata={},source='Expected: '+lab['output']+'\n\n## Your turn\n'+lab['task']),dict(cell_type='code',metadata={},source='',execution_count=None,outputs=[])]
  for n,c in enumerate(cells):c['id']=f'{name.lower()}-{n}'
  (sd/f'{name}.ipynb').write_text(json.dumps(dict(nbformat=4,nbformat_minor=5,metadata={'kernelspec':{'display_name':'Python 3','language':'python','name':'python3'}},cells=cells),indent=2))
  (ROOT/f'instructor_materials/ML/{name}_guide.md').write_text(body+f"\n## Tutor pacing and explanations\n\n10 minutes recall; 20 minutes paper example; 20 minutes slowly run the code; 10 minutes break; 30 minutes learner variation; 20 minutes individual explanation and repair; 10 minutes save and exit check. Total 120 minutes. Visit all five learners. Ask what each symbol and unit means before asking them to change it.\n\nReference: {lab['answer']}\n\nRepair: {lab['repair']}\n\nRecord complete / needs repair / absent. Carry unfinished repair into the next core session; defer its optional extension to protect foundation learning. Include this evidence in the existing week’s portfolio, without adding a second mark or increasing the assessment weight.\n")
  sequence.append(f"## Week {i}: {lab['after']} → {name} → next week's core lessons\n\n{lab['title']}. Output: {name}.ipynb with a worked result, individual variation and plain-language explanation.\n")
 sequence.append('## Readiness before ML13\n\nEach learner must explain a variable, index a list, read a row/column table, calculate a mean and proportion, and work through one weighted sum with units. Check individually using F01–F04. Use a repair example if a skill is missing; do not interpret attendance as understanding. Advanced calculus, matrix factorisation and deep learning remain later study.\n')
 text='\n'.join(sequence)
 (ROOT/'teaching/FOUNDATION_LABS.md').write_text(text)
 (ROOT/'student_materials/ML/FOUNDATION_LABS.md').write_text(text)
if __name__=='__main__':build()
